{"kind":"data","event":{"type":"tool_call","status":"complete","toolCallId":"01a1534c-fda7-779a-85b3-19bada412228","toolName":"task","args":{},"result":"{\"lg_name\":\"Command\",\"update\":{\"files\":{},\"messages\":[{\"lc\":1,\"type\":\"constructor\",\"id\":[\"langchain_core\",\"messages\",\"ToolMessage\"],\"kwargs\":{\"content\":\"## Findings\\n1. [Critical] unset-settler-burns-withdraw-credit — `withdraw()` can drop buyer refunds if `settler` is unset\\n   Criterion: 1 functional\\n   Location: /home/user/app/contracts/DemandPool.sol:DemandPool.withdraw (line approx 149)\\n   Why it matters: `withdraw()` always removes the buyer from `_buyers` and clears `_buyerIndex`, then calls `ISettler(settler).creditWithdrawal(...)`. If `settler` is still `address(0)`, this external call does not credit anyone, so the buyer loses their position and receives no claimable refund. This breaks the required withdraw flow and can permanently strand user funds in the pool.\\n   Suggested fix: Guard `withdraw()` (and optionally `commit()`/`claim`-path dependencies) with `if (!settlerSet) revert ...;` and/or `if (settler == address(0)) revert ...;` before mutating buyer state. Also consider wiring `settler` in the constructor to eliminate this initialization footgun.\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n- Assumed deployment target is Arc Testnet (chain 5042002), so the hardcoded USDC address `0x3600000000000000000000000000000000000000` is correct under criterion 3.\\n- Assumed permissionless `settle()` / `refundAll()` is intentional (not owner-restricted by design).\\n\\n## Audit Provenance\\n| Subagent | Dispatched | Files audited | Corpus rules cited |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| functional-auditor | yes | 4 | — |\",\"tool_call_id\":\"toolu_bdrk_013whNpiwdYPHrmKHVakNRpE\",\"name\":\"task\",\"additional_kwargs\":{},\"response_metadata\":{}}}]},\"goto\":[]}"}}
